[Music]  my mom Susan Kramer was born in 1949 in  Union New Jersey just outside of New  York City  her father supported this large family  by working two jobs 80 hours a week and  making less than $11,000 a year you know  like so many I think she wanted better  and she knew that she only had a shot  she saved money studied hard and stayed  driven so to save she started working  from 14 on in addition to going to  school she was studying hard as well  even though so many had the advice of  just getting married after graduation or  file into the one of few women okay  careers and those she respected all  these things they just burned her  passion she knew she won that  competitive edge by pursuing very  rigorous schooling ie and ideally but  most Ivy League's at the time were not  very accessible to women and they were  incredibly expensive  she found one exception which was  Cornell University so this is where she  knew she wanted to be headed and by the  middle of her senior year  she'd been accepted to Cornell  my mom loved Cornell it was challenging  diverse and completely invigorating but  with the academic workload she had been  unable to hold a profitable part-time  job like she had planned and by the  middle of her freshman year was  financially drained and confronted with  the reality that she may not be able to  pay for her next year she may have to  drop out and then on one of the most  serendipitous days of her life in fact  just hours after she had finally told  her friends that she's got to drop out  and disappoint her family and even more  so herself she was called to the  financial aid office for a meeting and  informed that she was the proud  recipient of a new scholarship from an  unlikely group the Tompkins Women's Club  so she learned from the aid office that  this rural group of women was comprised  of mothers and wives from a small town a  few miles down from the University this  group was not wealthy by any means but  just simply invigorated by the new tide  of women's education and they wanted to  be a part of it and though the Women's  Club knew that their contribution might  be small in comparison to others he  simply just wanted to do what they could  and for my mom  this gesture was anything but small it  was literally life-changing this  scholarship enabled me to stay at  Cornell these women did not know me at  all it was through their efforts that I  was able to finish Cornell and then go  on with my career but the momentum of  the school year had spooked my mama and  it wasn't until sometime later she even  had a moment to stop and think about  these women again get by them the con  and Phil had changed and additional  information lost my mom was never able  to track them down and she still regrets  to this day that she was unable to say  thank you  however today my mom personally funds  her own scholarship program and pays it  forward in every way she can she helps  reinforce that while hardwork is a must  sometimes you do just need a little bit  of help and her story also stands to  remind me that I can make a difference  and even perhaps a bigger difference  than I'll know because that ripple  effect of kindness is such a real thing  [Applause]  [Music]  you  Oh

